findings.body:
**Claimed critical; verifier found:** verifier off

Any attacker-controlled page can send a cross-origin request with its own `Origin`; this helper now echoes that value as `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` while always setting `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`. Browsers will expose the response to that origin and attach cookies on the request, so a malicious site can invoke cookie-authenticated API endpoints and read the result.

Native apps do not use CORS; if webviews need more hosts, extend the allowlist (or a strict suffix/pattern check) instead of reflecting arbitrary origins.

```suggestion
const ALLOWED = new Set(["https://app.example.com"]);

export function corsHeaders(origin: string | null): Record<string, string> {
  if (origin && ALLOWED.has(origin)) {
    return {
      "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": origin,
      "Access-Control-Allow-Credentials": "true",
      Vary: "Origin",
    };
  }
  return { Vary: "Origin" };
}
```
